{quote}I suppose we need to change the way we update to user {{update .. where id = x}} right? (to use numeric primary key){quote}

No, updating using unique index should be fine, it does not need any query optimisation. Table doesn’t even have that many indexes, which slows down writes:

{noformat}  PRIMARY KEY (`id`) /*T![clustered_index] CLUSTERED */,
  UNIQUE KEY `idx_request_id` (`request_id`),
  KEY `idx_last_update` (`last_update`)
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_general_ci AUTO_INCREMENT=118496783 {noformat}

Do you see this slow updates as a regular pattern thing or was it one off?

I also see some queries like 

{noformat}DELETE FROM `salt` WHERE last_update < DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 7 DAY) LIMIT 1000;{noformat}

that take seconds. not sure how these can be optimised because we also have an index on last_update

When D is registered as a legal entity then VAT applies only if D is VAT-obliged. To be eligible for VAT depends on Ds annual income. That part is missing in the article. It means some legal entities are VAT obliged and some are not.

That is why I suggest rephrasing the sentence: {color:#172b4d}“VAT is charged on Bolt trips if the driver is registered as a legal entity and VAT-obligated."{color}
{color:#172b4d}{color}

