[~accountid:5aab754b5c335d2a73a795f4] In my opinion, the *User* being *at fault* in an accident should be suggesting the harshest of outcomes possible and can always be adjusted through *Deviant Action* if needed. 

{color:#97a0af}In regards to {color}{color:#97a0af}*Poor Road Conditions*{color}{color:#97a0af} and this specific case, I’ve looked through the outcomes of past events logged under User Injured due to Adverse Weather or Poor Road Conditions to see how it has been handled. Seemingly similar reports were closed mostly with both {color}{{User At Fault}}{color:#97a0af} and {color}{{Cannot Be Confirmed}}{color:#97a0af}. Theoretically, the party that got into an accident due to poor road conditions can file a legal claim against the government (third party?). Generally, we will never know if the incident occurred because of the environment or due to not paying enough attention, hence {color}{{Cannot Be Confirmed}}{color:#97a0af} seems more appropriate to use. If clearly stated otherwise – it’s already a different event category, and we can possibly talk about Reckless Riding.{color}

To sum up, it seems to me that similarly to behavioral categories implying {{User At Fault}}, non-behavioral ones on default suggest no fault of the user. Unless there is some very specific situation. I think that {{User at Fault}} should be used cautiously, and for this exact reason, we made it possible to select {{Cannot be Confirmed}} for the multiple reports received.

